{
  "abstract": "We study rational Diophantine sextuples containing an antipodal pair in the exterior orbit of the classical order-three construction. We give an explicit necessary-and-sufficient description using four individual Pythagorean square conditions. For rational $\\lambda,z$, put $w=(\\lambda+3z)/(1+\\lambda z)$ and $m=-\\lambda w/z$. The conditions that $1+\\lambda^2$, $1+z^2$, $1+w^2$, and $1+m^2$ are separately rational squares supply three rational anchors, the antipodal pair, a sixth entry, and all fifteen individual pair roots. We give the rational formulas, all pole guards, and a short complete root table. Conversely, every ordinary rational sextuple on the stated interface admits these coordinates. A direct inverse uses only signed anchor roots satisfying $1/r+1/s=1/t$, and a rational pole argument covers every such input. Three of the four gates are exactly the individual roots joining the anchors to a distinguished common neighbour; paying them also splits the antipodal quadratic. Exact symbolic certificates, an independent rational anchor replay, and radical-algebra controls support the formulas. A fixed finite pilot produces only pole points after its stated prefilters. The contribution is an explicit equivalence and root-preserving reduction within the established order-three mechanism. We supply no ordinary rational point on the full four-circle cover, no seventh-entry construction, and no global nonexistence claim.",
